---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/loyalty';
const title = "Loyalty email: make earned rewards usable";
const desc = "Connect membership, balances, tier changes and redemption to a clear customer promise. Choose an email app by how reliably it can communicate your actual program rules, not by a generic loyalty ranking.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should an enrollment invitation send?",
    "answer": "At an entry point that matches the published program and customer intent. Compare checkout, explicit signup and repeat-purchase invitations rather than requiring every store to wait for a second order."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are loyalty tiers the same as VIP segments?",
    "answer": "No. A loyalty tier comes from program rules; a VIP segment can describe customer behavior. Reconcile benefits when a customer belongs to both."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which app is best for loyalty email?",
    "answer": "Use a demonstration of your ledger events, redemption rules, channels and failure recovery. This guide does not report a tested supplier winner."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Workflow guide</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Define the program before the invitation</h2>
      <p>Write down eligibility, earning events, redemption choices, tier qualification and any expiry rules. Distinguish joining the program from agreeing to marketing. A buyer who is eligible but not enrolled should not receive a balance as if an account already exists.</p>
      <p>An invitation can follow checkout, a repeat order or an explicit request, depending on the program. Compare those entry points against enrollment friction and message load; second purchase is a possible experiment, not a universal eligibility rule. Explain both how to earn and how to use a reward, with a direct enrollment destination.</p>
      <h2>Keep the ledger authoritative</h2>
      <p>Give each member a stable identifier shared with the rewards system. Define balance, redeemable balance, pending points, tier and last-updated time separately. Returns, cancelled orders and reversed referrals need correction events, not only positive earning events.</p>
      <p>Before sending, resolve the current state or omit the balance when freshness is uncertain. A delayed earning event should not overwrite a newer redemption. For duplicate and out-of-order events, record an event identifier and version so a retry cannot celebrate the same milestone twice.</p>
      <h2>Celebrate the perk the member can actually use</h2>
      <p>A tier upgrade email should explain the newly available benefit, how to claim it and any restrictions. Confirm the perk is active before sending. A downgrade notice needs the applicable qualification period and a support route for disputed activity.</p>
      <p>Keep loyalty tier separate from a behavioral VIP segment. If one customer qualifies for both, describe the combined benefits consistently instead of issuing contradictory status messages. Do not tighten an existing qualification promise merely because the top tier has grown.</p>
      <h2>Warn about the right expiring points</h2>
      <p>Read the expiring portion and its deadline from the program, including the relevant time zone. A total balance is not necessarily the amount about to expire. Recheck redemption, extension and account changes before any queued warning.</p>
      <p>Choose reminder lead time around the actual redemption process. A reward requiring a shipped purchase needs different preparation from an immediately usable benefit. Explain what survives the deadline; do not manufacture urgency or characterize every expiration message as exempt from marketing preferences.</p>
      <h2>Make redemption work at checkout</h2>
      <p>Link to eligible rewards or the account page and explain minimum spend, excluded products and whether points can combine with a code. Rehearse a member with insufficient points, a partially redeemed balance and a reward removed from the catalog.</p>
      <p>Show a support path for a failed redemption without asking the customer to disclose credentials. When points appear in a replenishment reminder, the reward must still apply to that product and market. A visible but unusable balance is a service issue to fix before increasing reminder frequency.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate points, promotions and referrals</h2>
      <p>Maintain an offer register covering points multipliers, sale prices, coupons and referral awards. Decide which combinations are permitted from actual contribution costs and program terms. Wholesale and employee eligibility should follow those terms rather than an automatic blanket exclusion.</p>
      <p>For referrals, define who earns which reward, the qualifying action and what happens after a return. A review reward should not depend on positive sentiment. Keep referral orders separate from ordinary member reorders when assessing acquisition value.</p>
      <h2>Reconcile stores and online purchases</h2>
      <p>If retail locations participate, map member identities and earning rules across channels. Decide how an offline sale reaches the ledger and how long reconciliation takes. Staff should explain the membership benefit and record communication preferences separately.</p>
      <p>Account merges and changed email addresses need an owner. Ask support to reconcile a disputed purchase without creating a second member record. Preserve the earning history during a platform change and compare balances before enabling messages from the replacement sender.</p>
      <h2>Measure the program without selection bias</h2>
      <p>Track invitations delivered, completed enrollments, usable rewards, redemption failures, repeat orders and support contacts. Report the denominator and observation window for each rate. Enrolled customers may already be more loyal than nonmembers.</p>
      <p>An enrolled-versus-nonenrolled comparison is descriptive. For an incentive or reminder experiment, define comparable eligible groups and the treatment before sending. Have finance set the appropriate accounting treatment for outstanding rewards; do not equate every issued point with deferred revenue or every expiration with profit.</p>
      <h2>Ask an app to demonstrate the hard cases</h2>
      <p>Request a demonstration using a returned order, duplicate tier event, stale balance, partial expiry and a POS purchase. Record which system owns the ledger, how errors surface and whether a marketer can pause only the affected loyalty path. These are proposed checks, not results from a supplier evaluation.</p>
      <p>Budget for rewards integration, message volume, reward cost, reconciliation and support time. Compare extending the current stack with replacing it. Preserve historical balances and outstanding promises during cutover; activate one owner per message so retries do not duplicate notices.</p>
      <h2>Release and review with named owners</h2>
      <p>Start with a reconciled member set and a record of expected messages. Rehearse earning, redemption, return reversal, upgrade, downgrade and expiry on controlled accounts. Verify the received email and destination as well as the event log.</p>
      <p>After release, review missing or stale balances, failed redemptions and message collisions. Revisit templates when program rules change. Pause the affected campaign if it cannot explain a valid benefit; keep support informed about rewards already promised.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related workflows">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">VIP segments →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Discount strategy →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment →</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
